      "claim": "Acamprosate, a small highly polar and fully ionized sulfonic acid with low passive permeability, has an absolute oral bioavailability of ~11% in fasting humans; this is the most transferable evidence because the query shares extreme polarity, full ionization at physiological pH, and very low estimated logD.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "Ferulic acid, a polar cinnamic acid, shows only 3–10% oral bioavailability in humans, providing direct human evidence that carboxylic-acid-containing polar molecules can fall well below the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C32"],
      "claim": "Tegaserod has ~10% absolute oral bioavailability in fasting humans, reinforcing that compounds with poor absorption can be low even when the chemical class differs; its lower structural relevance is offset by the consistent low-F direction.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C33"],
      "claim": "Microcystin-LR, a cyclic peptide with multiple polar groups, is reported to have low oral bioavailability in humans, supporting the idea that cyclic peptide-like polar scaffolds can be poorly absorbed; transferability is limited but direction is supportive.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C31"],
      "claim": "L-arginine, a zwitterionic amino acid, has ~70% oral bioavailability in humans, showing that polarity alone does not guarantee low F when active transport is available; however, the query is a large dicarboxylic acid cyclic peptide without the relevant cationic amino acid transporter, so this evidence has low transferability.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "Amoxicillin and clavulanate, polar beta-lactams, show high oral bioavailability (50–90%) via peptide transporter-mediated absorption; although the query has peptide-like bonds, its diketopiperazine core and two free carboxylates make it an unlikely PEPT1 substrate, and the transfer score is very low.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a highly polar, fully ionized dicarboxylic acid cyclic dipeptide with very low estimated logD and high TPSA. The most transferable experimental evidence—acamprosate (F≈11%), ferulic acid (3–10%), and tegaserod (≈10%)—all show low oral bioavailability in humans, consistent with poor passive permeability. High-F polar comparators (arginine, beta-lactams) rely on specific active transport not expected for this dicarboxylic acid DKP. Therefore, F≥20% is unlikely; predict low.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental bioavailability data for the query molecule.",
    "No permeability or transporter assay data for the query; possible active uptake by peptide transporters is uncertain.",
    "The query is a cyclic dipeptide; cyclic peptide permeability can be unpredictable and may differ from linear polar acids.",
    "No data on formulation, solubility, or efflux for the query."
  ],
